  in the wild, switching storage resets everyone's identifier and breaks continuity with whatever data you've already
  collected.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives tab close and browser restart, per-origin, no expiry until cleared. Gives you the longest
    correlation window across visits. JavaScript-only, so the server never sees it unless you explicitly send it.
    Cleared by "clear site data" and absent in private windows.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — lives for one tab. Two tabs get two different IDs and closing the tab loses it. That contradicts
    "persist," so I mention it only to rule it out.
  - [1mCookie[0m — sent automatically on every request to your origin, so the backend can correlate server-side without you
    passing anything. Costs: needs explicit [38;5;153mSameSite[39m/[38;5;153mSecure[39m/expiry flags to be set correctly, adds bytes to every
    request, and is the option most likely to be blocked or cleared by browser privacy controls.

  One factual note, not an objection: a persistent identifier that follows a person across visits is a tracking
  identifier, and under GDPR/ePrivacy that generally makes it personal data requiring a lawful basis and often consent —
  even when it's "just" a random UUID with no name attached. If this app has EU users, that's a conversation to have
  with whoever owns privacy; it doesn't change the code much, but it may add a consent check before minting. Flagging it
  so it's a decision rather than an accident.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.[0m It matches "persist" literally, it's the simplest correct implementation, and since
  your [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is still a stub there's no server-side correlation to benefit from a cookie yet. If a real backend
  later wants server-side visibility, you can send the value explicitly in the request body without changing where it's
  stored.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Storage [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mWhere should the anonymous ID be stored, and how long should it live?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mlocalStorage (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mSurvives browser restarts, per-origin, no expiry. Simplest correct option; server sees it only if you send it [39m
     [38;5;246mexplicitly.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Cookie
     [38;5;246mSent automatically to the server on every request. Needs SameSite/Secure/expiry flags. More likely to be cleared or
[39m     [38;5;246mblocked.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m sessionStorage
     [38;5;246mOne tab only, lost on close. Listed for completeness — this does not satisfy 'persist'.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
